items.description:
The six authored cells split 3 volatile-keyed / 3 calm-keyed:
  VOLATILE-keyed: trending/volatile trend_donchian{long:off}; trending/volatile ict_scalp_5m{long,short:off}; chop/volatile ict_scalp_5m{long,short:off}
  CALM-keyed:     trending/calm squeeze_breakout_4h{short:off}; transitional/calm trend_donchian{long:off}; chop/calm trend_donchian{long:off}

The live BTC ML vol label resolves VOLATILE ~1% of the time (10/979 advisory rows; 40/40 sampled live regime_hard_gate rows read calm). So the three volatile-keyed cells can fire on ~1% of bars and the three calm-keyed cells carry essentially all the enforcement. The vol axis, as SERVED, is close to a constant — a 2-D gate whose second axis is ~99% one value is barely 2-D.

This does NOT say the cells are wrong. They were authored from a vol-SPLIT of BTC history where volatile was a meaningful share, and the A/B that justified them (docs/research/A-vol-gating-OFFcell-design-2026-06-27.md) lifted the book 4.3x. The question this raises is whether the LIVE label reproduces the label the cells were authored under — which is exactly the open parity gap.

CONSEQUENCE FOR GRADING (why the cells are still ungraded): grading them off the OFFLINE replay's vol labels would grade a COUNTERFACTUAL — the replay says volatile 9.1% of bars, the live gate ~1%. Cells must be graded off the LIVE gate's own recorded labels (regime_hard_gate rows carry vol_regime + vol_cell + vol_enforced), not off replayed labels.

items.source:
MEASURED 2026-08-13, trainer relays #8854/#8855, round at merged main bf398d5 against the CORRECT journal (data/trade_journal.db, 4585 trades).

The --db correction worked: live trades load now (they did not before). But runtime_logs/m20_exit_head/scalp_15m/build_report.json shows where they went:
    trades_in: 1102   skipped: {no_candles: 701, unresolvable: 39}
    families: donchian        {live: 3}    rows_by_year {2026: 1753}
              ict_scalp_xrp_15m {harness: 353}  <- the leg being graded: live ABSENT
              pullback        {live: 6}    rows_by_year {2026: 1865}
and all three e1_reports still read live=0.

TWO DISTINCT DEFECTS, deliberately not merged:

(1) THE LIVE ARM IS SELECTED BY INSTRUMENT, NOT BY LEG. The journal holds 14 ict_scalp_xrp_15m rows (11 closed), yet that family got live=0 while `donchian` and `pullback` families -- which this scalp round never asked for -- got 3 and 6. family_of() buckets on the strategy NAME, so same-symbol strategies (xrp_pullback_2h etc.) are pulled in and bucketed into families of their own. The scalp leg's own live rows were then dropped, most plausibly by the 701 no_candles skips (m27_data coverage vs the live trade window), but that is the NEXT thing to measure, not something to assert here.

(2) A CRASH REPORTS SUCCESS. Training the spurious `pullback` family (0 harness rows) died with `ValueError: Expected 2D array, got 1D array instead: array=[]` and the round still printed `ROUND tf=15m exit=0`. A future session reading exit=0 concludes every family trained. This is the same shape as the earlier `no trades loaded` runs that looked fine.

MEASURED 2026-08-13 (diag #9053) while trying to live-verify BL-20260813-ICTSCALP-BTC-BYBIT2-BALANCE-REJECTS. Part B (PR #9039) added notes.margin_basis so a rejected row would name the basis it was sized from 'without a diag round-trip'. Read against the 8 newest post-deploy rows (ids 4624-4631, 2026-08-13T16:14Z-17:01Z), the key is absent on ALL of them — and absent is uninformative by construction.

Three different conditions produce the identical empty field:
  (a) the row rejected BEFORE sizing (intent-layer noop) — 4 of 8 rows;
  (b) sizing ran but hit one of SIX `return 0.0` early exits in risk.py::position_size (lines 952, 1053, 1055, 1067, 1082, 1090),       every one of which precedes the margin_basis_out write at 1223-1226 — 2 of 8 rows (alpaca_live sized_qty=0);
  (c) the stamp is genuinely broken.
Nothing in the row distinguishes them. Establishing it was (a)/(b) and not (c) took reading coordinator.py:1732 and six early-exit sites in risk.py — i.e. exactly the diag-and-code round-trip the field was introduced to eliminate.

items.detail:
Hit four times in a row while rendering this run's report. The skill's prose says a relay-blocked block is recorded as 'an explicit "unavailable: <reason>" string' and describes strategy_promotion / ml_training_health / soak_status as narrative assessments. The renderer requires structured objects and calls .get() on them unguarded:
  * review_coverage.execution_capture -> dict with per_strategy[] + anomalies[]  (line ~779 and ~415)
  * review_coverage.strategy_promotion -> dict with ready_to_promote[] / demote_or_kill[] / summary  (line ~395)
  * review_coverage.ml_training_health -> dict with cycles_since_last_review / dataset_builds_ok / summary
  * review_coverage.soak_status -> LIST of {soak, state, detail}
  * health.findings -> dict-of-dicts keyed by finding name (line ~159)
  * consolidated.operator_priorities -> {rank, title, source_review, tier, detail, operator_action_required}
  * consolidated.tier3_proposals_pending -> {source_review, summary, tier}
Following the SKILL as written produces a payload that CANNOT render, and the failure mode is a raw AttributeError ('str' object has no attribute 'get') with no indication of which key is wrong - so each shape has to be rediscovered by reading the renderer source one crash at a time. The authoritative shape is comms/schema/system_report_response.template.json, which the skill does not point at for these sub-blocks.
